WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.490 --> 00:00:04.405
Your Spark cluster runs all night, and sits idle all day.

2
00:00:04.425 --> 00:00:06.330
You pay for machines that do nothing.

3
00:00:06.350 --> 00:00:08.990
And every team wants its own version.

4
00:00:09.010 --> 00:00:14.565
This port has three steps: operators, Spark and Airflow, and a lane for batch.

5
00:00:14.585 --> 00:00:17.275
First: teach the cluster a new object.

6
00:00:17.295 --> 00:00:20.175
A custom resource definition adds a new kind.

7
00:00:20.195 --> 00:00:22.985
For example: a Spark application.

8
00:00:23.005 --> 00:00:25.610
An operator is a controller for that kind.

9
00:00:25.630 --> 00:00:28.360
It does the manual steps for you.

10
00:00:28.380 --> 00:00:30.845
Second: one pod per piece of work.

11
00:00:30.865 --> 00:00:33.445
Each Spark job gets its own driver pod.

12
00:00:33.465 --> 00:00:36.205
The driver asks for executor pods.

13
00:00:36.225 --> 00:00:38.330
When the job ends, the pods go away.

14
00:00:38.350 --> 00:00:40.240
You pay for the run only.

15
00:00:40.260 --> 00:00:44.690
Airflow can do the same: every task runs in its own pod.

16
00:00:44.710 --> 00:00:47.300
Third: keep batch away from serving.

17
00:00:47.320 --> 00:00:51.735
Use a tainted node pool, a low priority, and a quota for each team.

18
00:00:51.755 --> 00:00:53.923
A taint only keeps other pods out.

19
00:00:53.943 --> 00:00:57.010
Add a node selector to keep batch in.

20
00:00:57.030 --> 00:00:59.445
Quick recap of data platforms.

21
00:00:59.465 --> 00:01:01.232
Operators add new kinds.

22
00:01:01.252 --> 00:01:02.845
Jobs bring their own pods.

23
00:01:02.865 --> 00:01:04.980
And batch gets its own lane.

24
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:08.100
Now start the first lesson below.
